# Line endings are git's business, not this file's. Nothing here converts a file on checkout, so a
# working tree holds whatever the platform's own configuration produces — CRLF on a stock Windows
# install, LF elsewhere. The golden comparisons are indifferent to which: they treat CR, LF and
# CRLF as one line terminator, so a file and the string a renderer built compare equal whichever
# form the checkout wrote. `src/golden.rs` and `tests/common` each hold that comparison.
#
# What is left below are the files that are not text at all, and the files whose exact bytes are
# the subject rather than the carrier.

# Zip containers and a PDF: any conversion corrupts them outright. Git's own heuristic reaches the
# same conclusion, and saying so removes the question. Named by extension with no directory,
# because workbooks live under `docs/` and under `tests/fixtures/` both.
*.xlsx binary
*.pdf binary

# The app icon and the interface faces: the icon is embedded into the Windows executables by
# build.rs, and both fonts into the GUI binary by `include_bytes!`. `**` rather than `*`, because
# the fonts sit a directory below the icon and a single `*` does not cross a `/`.
assets/** binary

# The Green Button feeds, held at the CRLF Toronto Hydro serves them with. `-text` is "convert
# nothing, in either direction": these are specimens cut from a real export, and normalising them
# would make the fixtures differ from the bytes the reader meets in the field.
#
# Nothing asserts this. The parser does not care what terminates a line, so a feed falling outside
# the pattern would be normalised on the next `git add` from a machine with `core.autocrlf` set,
# and no test anywhere would fail. That silence is why the pattern names no directory and spells
# the extension as a character class: a feed is covered wherever it is put and however its
# extension is capitalised, rather than only where the four that exist today happen to sit.
*.[xX][mM][lL] -text
